# The scenario engine's directives: what a fact document may say beside its example. The engine
# owns them and never renders them, so no graph holds a row about any of them; they are declared
# here so an editor and the documentation renderer read a document against them. A document states
# its own example whole and pulls in what it builds on with @withFacts; nothing is prepended to it.
#
# A document is a scenario, walked top to bottom: @withFacts renders documents into the scenario's
# folder, @tick moves its clock, the scenario's own definitions render where the first of them is
# written, every render is captured, and each claim is checked once against the capture before it.
# @withConfiguration is read from the scenario's own file only.

# Each assertion may narrow its relation with where: a SQL condition over the relation's own
# columns, applied as written before the comparison, so an equality can be stated over part of a
# relation that holds every element of the graph.
directive @expectEquals(relation: String!, where: String, rows: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA
directive @assertContains(relation: String!, where: String, rows: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA
directive @assertAbsent(relation: String!, where: String, rows: String!) repeatable on SCHEMA
# The relation holds no row: in this document's graph where the relation carries one, and in the
# store where it does not, as every assertion ranges.
directive @assertEmpty(relation: String!, where: String) repeatable on SCHEMA

# The configuration a graph is captured under, as a build would have handed it to the run: read by
# the fact harness off the document's text before the capture, since a reading is told its
# configuration rather than finding it. A document without it is a run that declared nothing. The
# output coordinates travel together, all three or none; the schema files are always the document's
# own. In a graph read in steps, a reading takes it from the files as they then stand.
#
# jooqPackage is the catalog the graph is captured against. Left out, it is the fixture catalog every
# other graph reads; jooqPackage: null is a run that declared no catalog. All graphs share one store,
# so a graph without the fixture catalog still sits beside the rows the others' reading of it wrote,
# which is what a claim on whose catalog a rule reads is tested against.
directive @withConfiguration(
  jooqPackage: String,
  supergraph: String,
  tenantColumn: String,
  outputPackage: String,
  outputJooqPackage: String,
  outputDirectory: String,
  lintDisabledRules: [String!],
  lintExcludedTypes: [String!],
  sessionState: Boolean,
  sessionHandle: String,
  maxAllowedPageSize: Int, outboxTable: String, outboxFunction: String, outboxColumns: [String!], outboxParameters: [String!], outboxPayload: String
) on SCHEMA

# @withFacts(docs: [{path: "layouts/anchor"}, {path: "shapes/film", as: "shapes/film"}]) renders
# each document, and first what it pulls in, into the scenario's folder as the file its entry names
# (the path where as is omitted), at the clock's time. A document pulled in contributes its example
# without its claims, the last version of each file it writes, and moves no clock.
directive @withFacts(docs: [FactDoc!]!) repeatable on SCHEMA
input FactDoc { path: String!, as: String }

# @tick(ms: 60000) moves the scenario's clock, which starts at the same instant for every scenario.
directive @tick(ms: Int!) repeatable on SCHEMA
